  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  name: satkit\n  version: \"0.22.0\"\n\npackage:\n  name: ${{ name|lower }}\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  # The PyPI sdist ships everything the build needs, including the data\n  # compiled into the extension (data/manifest.json and data/embedded/*.gz —\n  # see MANIFEST.in upstream). No network access is needed at build time.\n  url: https://pypi.org/packages/source/${{ name[0] }}/${{ name }}/${{ name }}-${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: d484d77ae0fa690efbb3bb052514c1b5a02f9ed28fb2cb9dd3f220bbe2e405e0\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  script:\n    # conda-forge policy for Rust packages: bundle the licenses of every crate\n    # compiled into the extension module. The sdist root is a cargo workspace\n    # (core crate + python/ bindings), so one run covers both.\n    - cargo-bundle-licenses --format yaml --output THIRDPARTY.yml\n    - ${{ PYTHON }} -m pip install . -vv --no-deps --no-build-isolation\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('rust') }}\n    - cargo-bundle-licenses\n  host:\n    - python\n    - pip\n    - setuptools\n    - setuptools-rust\n  run:\n    - python\n    - numpy >=1.0\n    # Since 0.21 the core data (IERS nutation tables, gravity models) is\n    # compiled into the extension and the JPL ephemeris is downloaded on first\n    # use, SHA-256 verified, so no data package is required. For offline\n    # environments populate a directory once with `satkit.utils.update_datafiles()`\n    # and point `SATKIT_DATA` at it, or host a mirror and set `SATKIT_DATA_URL`.\n\ntests:\n  # PyO3-`wrap_pymodule!` submodules (jplephem, frametransform, ...) are\n  # exposed as attributes on `satkit`, not separately-importable\n  # top-level modules — so only `satkit` itself goes in `imports:`.\n  - python:\n      imports:\n        - satkit\n  # Offline smoke test: an empty data directory, downloads forbidden\n  # (SATKIT_OFFLINE=1), no network — exercises time scales, gravity, the\n  # IERS precession-nutation tables, SGP4, and checks the ephemeris is NOT\n  # fetched. See run_test.py.\n  - files:\n      recipe:\n        - run_test.py\n    requirements:\n      run:\n        - numpy\n    script:\n      - python run_test.py\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://github.com/ssmichael1/satkit\n  summary: Satellite orbital mechanics library (SGP4, JPL ephemerides, frame transforms)\n  description: |\n    SatKit is a high-performance orbital mechanics library written in\n    Rust with complete Python bindings via PyO3. It handles coordinate\n    transforms, orbit propagation, time systems, gravity models,\n    atmospheric density, and JPL ephemerides — everything needed for\n    satellite astrodynamics work.\n\n    The IERS nutation tables and gravity models are compiled in, so the\n    package works with no data files and no network. The JPL DE440\n    ephemeris (~102 MB) is downloaded on first use, SHA-256 verified;\n    offline environments can pre-populate a data directory\n    (`satkit.utils.update_datafiles()`, then `SATKIT_DATA`) or point\n    `SATKIT_DATA_URL` at a mirror.\n  # The source is MIT OR Apache-2.0; the extension embeds the ITU_GRACE16\n  # gravity model (CC BY 4.0, truncated to degree 70) — see THIRDPARTY-DATA.md.\n  license: (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND CC-BY-4.0\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE-MIT\n    - LICENSE-APACHE\n    # Attribution / licence notice for the embedded datasets (in the sdist\n    # from 0.22.1; the copy in the recipe directory covers 0.22.0).\n    - THIRDPARTY-DATA.md\n    # Generated at build time by cargo-bundle-licenses (see build.script).\n    - THIRDPARTY.yml\n  documentation: https://satkit.dev/\n  repository: https://github.com/ssmichael1/satkit\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - ssmichael1\n",
